{
 "comment": "tds_payees, #1243: vouchers that share a GUID (blank, or one GUID repeated) are each their own bill. Transporter A (two blank-GUID bills, Rs 20,000 and Rs 25,000) and Transporter B (one GUID on the same two) are under the single sum and the aggregate: no finding. Transporter C: five blank-GUID bills of Rs 25,000 cross the aggregate on the fifth; the largest single bill is Rs 25,000, each bill is cited and each is its own clause 21(b) row. Carrier G: four blank-GUID bills on one day on a goods-carriage ledger, numbered so that the order of their labels differs from the order of the books, which their keys follow (the eighth to eleventh blank GUIDs: a place zero-padded, so the tenth and eleventh sort after the ninth), two of them with one number (one cited ref). Landlord D: Rs 30,000 of rent in May and in August, each month under the limit. Landlord E: May holds Rs 40,000 (blank GUID) and Rs 20,000 (its own GUID), August Rs 40,000 (blank GUID): the highest month is Rs 60,000. Transporter F: one GUID on three bills of one day, two of them with one number (one cited ref) and a third between them in the books with another; every amount is summed. Carrier K: two bills of one day, each over the single sum, a unique GUID read before a blank one: each is its own clause 21(b) row, numbered in key order (the blank GUID's key first), not in the books' order.",
